<mujoco model="track_b_b3_two_body_placeholder">
  <!--
    B3.1: Two independent free bodies, Track B (MuJoCo backend),
    extending b0_single_body_placeholder.xml for Track C's multi-agent
    coordination work.

    Purpose (B3.1's own scope, deliberately narrow): prove two
    independent free bodies can coexist in one MuJoCo scene at all, with
    contact constraints between them DISABLED for this first test. This
    deliberately isolates "does the solver couple two unrelated bodies"
    (a scene-wiring/indexing question, B3.2/B3.3's job) from "do the
    bodies collide as intended" (a physical-interaction question, B3.4,
    explicitly flagged as NOT required for the MVP). Answering both at
    once would leave an ambiguous result if something looked wrong: this
    file's structure means B3.2's zero-cross-talk test can only fail for
    scene-wiring reasons, never because contacts introduced unintended
    coupling.

    Contacts disabled via the GLOBAL flag contact="disable" (confirmed
    exact MJCF syntax directly against MuJoCo's own XML reference, and
    against a real MuJoCo test file using this identical pattern), not
    per-geom contype/conaffinity: the simplest thing to reason about with
    nothing else in the scene, and B3.4's own framing ("enabling real
    contact constraints... for free") implies flipping this one flag back
    to "enable" is the intended later path, not reconfiguring per-geom
    settings retroactively.

    Body indexing (confirmed via MuJoCo's own convention, index 0 is
    always the implicit world body; see mujoco_backend.py's own
    _BODY_ID=1 comment): body 1 = auv_placeholder, body 2 =
    auv_placeholder_2. B3.3's own position-indexing test depends on this
    exact numbering being stable and unsurprising.

    Body 1 (auv_placeholder) is UNCHANGED from
    b0_single_body_placeholder.xml: same name, position, inertial
    properties, geom, specifically so existing single-body code
    (mujoco_backend.py's hardcoded _BODY_ID=1, and every B0-B2 test built
    against it) continues to work completely unmodified when pointed at
    THIS file instead, with body 2 simply present but untouched by any
    code that only ever references index 1. This is exactly what B3.2's
    zero-cross-talk test needs: a true single-body baseline to compare
    against, not a new, differently-configured body 1.

    Body 2 (auv_placeholder_2) is structurally IDENTICAL to body 1 (same
    mass, inertia, geom size) but placed 2m away in x, far enough that the
    two geoms (each 0.3 x 0.15 x 0.15) never overlap even under
    substantial motion, so nothing about this test's result could be
    confused with a contact effect even before B3.4 revisits that
    question deliberately. Using an IDENTICAL body here, not a
    deliberately different one, is itself a design choice: B3.2 compares
    "body 2 actuated with a generic parameter set" against "the same
    generic parameters run standalone", so keeping body 2's own MJCF
    definition identical to body 1's means any observed divergence is
    attributable to the SCENE (two bodies present) rather than to some
    incidental difference between the two bodies' own placeholder
    properties.

    Neither body has hydrodynamics, damping, buoyancy, or cross-coupling
    wired in at the MJCF level, exactly as in the single-body file, since
    the full-override architecture (see mujoco_backend.py) injects all
    real, calibratable physics via Python at runtime, per body, not via
    anything declared here.
  -->
  <option gravity="0 0 -9.81">
    <flag contact="disable"/>
  </option>

  <worldbody>
    <body name="auv_placeholder" pos="0 0 0">
      <freejoint/>
      <!-- PLACEHOLDER inertial properties, see file header. Unchanged
           from b0_single_body_placeholder.xml. -->
      <inertial pos="0 0 0" mass="11.2" diaginertia="0.15 0.25 0.20"/>
      <geom type="box" size="0.3 0.15 0.15" rgba="0.2 0.5 0.8 1"/>
    </body>

    <body name="auv_placeholder_2" pos="2 0 0">
      <freejoint/>
      <!-- Structurally IDENTICAL to body 1's own placeholder properties,
           see file header for why this is deliberate, not laziness. -->
      <inertial pos="0 0 0" mass="11.2" diaginertia="0.15 0.25 0.20"/>
      <geom type="box" size="0.3 0.15 0.15" rgba="0.8 0.4 0.2 1"/>
    </body>
  </worldbody>
</mujoco>